{214} The Blessed and Exalted One informs that He will surely test His servants with prosperity, adversity and hardship, as He did with those before them. This is His established practice, which does not change or alter: whoever stands up for His religion and His law will surely be tried. If he is patient in obeying God, and does not care about the hardships standing in the path of it, then he is the truthful one, who has attained the perfection of happiness and the means of leadership. But whoever makes the trial of people equal to the punishment of God, such that hardships turn him away from what he is engaged in, and tribulations divert him from his goal, then he is the liar in his claim to faith. For faith is not mere adornment, wishful thinking and bare claims, until deeds either confirm it or refute it. The earlier nations experienced what God mentioned about them: {Misfortune and adversity afflicted them}, meaning poverty and sicknesses in their bodies, {and they were shaken}, by various kinds of fear, from threats of killing and exile, the seizure of wealth, the killing of loved ones, and various kinds of harm, until their condition reached such a point, and the upheaval brought them to the point where they considered God's help slow in coming, despite their certainty in it. But because of the severity and tightness of the matter, they said: {When will God's help come?} Then, when relief came at the height of hardship, and whenever the matter became constricted it became expanded, God the Exalted said: {Indeed, God's help is near}. Thus it is with everyone who stands for the truth: he will be tested; whenever it intensifies and becomes difficult for him, if he persists and perseveres in what he is upon, the trial is transformed for him into a gift, the hardships into comforts, and this is followed by victory over the enemies and the healing of the disease in his heart.

This verse is similar to God's saying: {Or do you think that you will enter Paradise without God knowing those of you who have striven and knowing the patient?} And His saying: {Alif, Lam, Mim. Do people think that they will be left to say, "We believe," and they will not be tried? And We had certainly tried those before them, and God will surely make evident those who are truthful and will surely make evident the liars.}

Thus, at the time of trial a person is either honored or humiliated.
